WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.000
How can an inhibitory input stop a cell from firing, while leaving its resting

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.000
voltage unchanged?

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:11.520
Start with the balance point.

4
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.640
It is the voltage the cell settles at, if its open channels stay as they are.

5
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.440
A marker on a voltage scale shows the membrane potential.

6
00:00:21.483 --> 00:00:25.843
Every kind of open channel has a rope, tied at its own reversal potential.

7
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:28.703
Each rope pulls the marker toward its end.

8
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:33.777
How hard a rope pulls is its current: conductance times driving force,

9
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:37.837
Conductance is mostly how many channels are open.

10
00:00:38.837 --> 00:00:41.817
Driving force is the gap between the marker and the rope's end.

11
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:47.067
In our model, one rope, the leak, stands in for the channels open at rest.

12
00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:51.527
It is 10 nS, tied at minus −65 mV.

13
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.140
Now a glutamate synapse fires.

14
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.880
Its AMPA receptors add a second rope, tied near 0 mV.

15
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.430
The marker is pulled up, but the leak holds it back.

16
00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.790
So it settles between the two ropes.

17
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.120
That rise is a postsynaptic potential.

18
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.300
When the channels close, the new rope goes slack.

19
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.640
The marker drifts back to rest.

20
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:16.953
GABA_A receptors are chloride channels.

21
00:01:16.953 --> 00:01:21.033
Where their rope is tied depends on the cell's chloride gradient.

22
00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:23.207
Here is a question.

23
00:01:24.127 --> 00:01:27.007
A GABA rope is tied at minus −75 mV.

24
00:01:27.727 --> 00:01:31.767
The cell rests at minus −65 Which way does the marker move?

25
00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:43.610
Down, toward minus −75 The rope is tied below the marker, so the balance point drops

26
00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.870
below rest. That is the textbook IPSP.

27
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:50.853
An EPSP makes the cell more likely to fire.

28
00:01:51.753 --> 00:01:53.853
An IPSP makes firing less likely.

29
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:58.713
The definition is about firing, not about which way the trace moves.

30
00:01:59.716 --> 00:02:04.896
So this lesson will show an IPSP that leaves a resting cell's voltage unchanged.

31
00:02:05.836 --> 00:02:10.176
And a GABA input that moves the voltage up, yet can still hold back excitation.

32
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.590
Written out, the balance point is an average of the ropes' ends.

33
00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:18.410
This holds for a passive cell, with one voltage everywhere.

34
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.000
Each end is weighted by that rope's share of the total conductance.

35
00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.720
It also assumes no current injected through an electrode, and conductances that do

36
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:30.860
not change with voltage.

37
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.940
The marker then chases this point over time.

38
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:36.833
So a new synapse does two jobs.

39
00:02:37.713 --> 00:02:40.193
First, it pulls the balance point toward its own end.

40
00:02:41.116 --> 00:02:43.536
Second, it adds to the total conductance.

41
00:02:44.356 --> 00:02:47.736
Every other rope's share shrinks, so every other rope counts for less.

42
00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:53.286
In this passive cell, the voltage also settles faster when a synapse opens.

43
00:02:54.266 --> 00:02:55.546
The next chapter shows why.

44
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.140
A synapse is one more rope on the balance point.

45
00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.900
Conductances add. The balance point is their weighted average.
